// expect-exit 2
//
// An index past the end of a list must not answer 0.
//
// `orbit_list_get` returned an error Result and the emitter threw the Result
// away: `_lr.ok ? *(void**)_lr.value : NULL`. A read past the end and a slot
// that really holds 0 were the same value, so a caller had nothing to check
// and nothing to notice. `xs.get(99)` over a two-element list printed 0.
//
// What it answers now is a diagnostic and a stop. There is no `option` in the
// language to shape an answer as, and any sentinel would be the same problem
// again: a caller cannot tell a stored 0 from a missing one, which is the whole
// reason this is a stop rather than a value.
//
//     $ orbit run tests/suite/list_index_out_of_range.orb
//     orbit: list index out of range
//       index    99
//       length   2
//       elements int
//       in       orbit_main (<build>:NN)
//
// Exit 2 is what orbit_list_index_error exits with. The exit code is the whole
// assertion: the static half of this check -- a LITERAL index into a LITERAL
// receiver -- is an error[E1002] at compile time and lives in
// tests/negative/n32_at_out_of_range.orb. This file is the half the front end
// cannot see, where the length is only known at run time, so the program has to
// be built and run for the gate to mean anything.
fn main() -> int {
    var xs = []
    xs.push(1)
    xs.push(2)
    print(xs.len())
    print(xs.get(99))
    // Unreachable. If the read answers anything at all -- 0, NULL, an address
    // -- the line above has already printed a value and this one prints a
    // second, and the exit code is 0 and the gate fails.
    print("unreachable")
    return 0
}
